      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We need to determine, for positive integers \\( n \\) and \\( m \\), the number of ways to partition a day into \\( n \\) equal \\( m \\)-second segments. This is equivalent to finding how many ways there are to write \\( 86400 \\) in the form \\( n \\cdot m \\), i.e., finding the number of positive integer pairs \\( (n, m) \\) such that:\n\n\\[\nn \\cdot m = 86400\n\\]\n\nTo accomplish this, we follow the steps below:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of seconds in a day**: \n   \\[\n   86400 \\text{ seconds}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the distribution range for \\( n \\) and \\( m \\)**:\n   Because \\( m \\) (the number of seconds per portion) must be a divisor of \\( 86400 \\), \\( m \\) can be any divisor or multiple of the divisors of \\( 86400 \\). As each \\( m \\) divisors of \\( 86400 \\) represents a unique \\( n \\).\n\n3. **List the count of all divisors of \\( 86400 \\)** (though generally, direct listing isn’t practical for such large values):\n\n4. For each divisor \\( m \\) of \\( 86400 \\), it corresponds uniquely to an \\( n \\) via \\( n \\cdot m = 86400 \\).\n\nLet’s find the number of divisors of \\( 86400 \\) via its prime factorization:\n\nFirst, decompose \\( 86400 \\) into its prime factors:\n\n\\[\n86400=2^{10} \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe formula to find the number of divisors \\( d(n) \\) of a number \\( n \\) given its prime factorization \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\ldots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n\n\\[\nd(n) = (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\ldots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nApplying this to \\( 86400 \\):\n\n\\[\nd(86400) = (10 + 1)(3 + 1)(2 + 1) = 11 \\times 4 \\times 3\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd(86400) = 132\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( 86400 \\) has 132 divisors.\n\n5. Since each unique divisor \\( m \\) uniquely influences \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of ways to partition a day into \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) seconds} = 132\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe number of ways for a day to be evenly divided into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{132}\n\\]",
