      "text": "We need to find how many ways to divide a day into intervals of seconds.\nA day can be divided into intervals of the form $k$ seconds for some integer $k$. There are 60 such intervals in an hour, and 24 hours in a day, so we have $60 \\times 24 = 1,440$ total intervals.\nNow we need to find how many ways we can choose pairs of intervals $(n,m)$ such that $n$ is the number of intervals in the $m$th day of the year and $m$ is the number of seconds in each interval.\nThere are $60 \\times 24 = 1,440$ intervals in a day, and each interval represents a position in the sequence of intervals. So the number of ways to divide a day into $n$ periods of $m$ seconds is $n(n-1)/2$.\nTherefore, there are $1440$ ways to divide a day into $n$ periods of $m$ seconds.",
