      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Notice that we want an equal number of seconds in every period, so $m$ must be a divisor of $86400$. Also, each period must be at least one second long, so $m \\leq 86400$. The number of divisors of $86400$ less than or equal to $86400$ is the number of ways we can divide a day into $n$ periods of $m$ seconds. So, we want to count the divisors of $86400=2^7\\cdot3^3\\cdot5^2$, and then remove $1$ because we do not want to consider the case $m = n = 1$.\nSince $1$ has only one divisor, namely $1$, then $86400$ minus $1$ is the answer, so $86400-1=86399$. It might be easier to do the following:\n\\[86400=2^7\\times3^3\\times5^2\\ge3\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty10^{-n}\\]\n\nWe want to split the sum so that the number of $1$'s ending in $0$ is twice the number of $1$'s ending in $5$. To satisfy this, for each term $$k=1k'-1k''k''',100k'+1000\\leq k'\\leq k''\\leq \\frac{86399}{5^2}-k'''''=250,$$\nfor each of the $10$ possible values of $k,k',k''$, we have exactly one way we can split the sum up. The first of these is $k=1,$ where we have $$100k'+1000\\leq 250-250,$$\nand only one possibility satisfies. Fortunately for us, $$25+10\\leq 25+25\\leq \\frac{8399}{25}<40,$$\nso we don't have to push as hard for the next $k'$ or $k''$. For each of the $20\\times 20=400$ choices of $k'.$ we have $\\boxed{200}$ ways to split the sum.\nFinally, the same argument works for any power of 2. Note that $$86400\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty2^{-n}=(86400+24-24)\\times\\frac12=\\boxed43200,$$\nbut in this case, we don't have to do as much maintenance for the next rung up. We can do the backward argument if this is easy to finish. From an argument given here, we see that of the $100$ possible runs, $88$ are in the form $$8\\cdots5k',32\\cdots5k'''.$$\nFrom these we get $$\\boxed{88}\\times\\boxed{10}=880.$$\nSimilarly, $$2^3\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty3^{-n}=60,$$\nso $$2^3\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty2^{-mn}=260.$$\nNext we do $$2^6\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty2^{-n}=(8+24-24)\\times\\frac12=12,$$ so $$2^6\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty2^{-nm}=12\\times26=156.$$\nThis is exactly what we cooperated, so $$\\boxed{43200}.$$",
